10:15:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That matches normal experience: virtually all cars made for decades have been **negative ground**. If you work on ordinary modern vehicles, it is safe to treat “car ground” as battery negative. The exceptions are real but historical—not something you would expect to encounter on modern cars. Some pre-1960s British vehicles, a few early American vehicles, and some tractors used **positive-ground** electrical systems. They disappeared because negative ground became the industry standard, especially with alternators and modern electronics. So the practical answer is: - **Modern car:** chassis/body = battery negative. - **Electron flow:** battery negative → chassis → electrical load → positive wiring → battery positive. - **Why call it ground?** The chassis is the shared electrical return/reference conductor; it is not Earth ground. - **Why negative?** Standardization, compatibility, and the convenience of using the body as the common return path. Your experience is exactly what the current automotive world looks like.
